Checked the changed surface in [src/metal/metal_server.cpp](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/src/metal/metal_server.cpp:455), [health handler](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/src/metal/metal_server.cpp:1228), and [trace boot event](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/src/metal/metal_server.cpp:1214). The `/health` change is additive, and in-repo consumers either ignore the body/status-only check or parse only `status == "ok"`: examples are [tools/reqlog_gate.sh](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/tools/reqlog_gate.sh:97), [tools/interleave_gate.sh](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/tools/interleave_gate.sh:134), and [tools/thunderdome_pin_ab.sh](/Users/macthedan/projects/q27/tools/thunderdome_pin_ab.sh:33).

The `&runtime` capture is safe: `runtime` is constructed before `server`, `server.listen(...)` blocks, and reverse destruction order destroys `server` before `runtime` after listen returns. `model_name` is initialized before serving and is only read afterward.

Nuance on “cannot throw”: `std::filesystem::path(model).filename()` will not throw because the input string is an arbitrary/nonexistent path; it does not stat or validate an artifact. The expression is not formally `noexcept` because string/path construction can still allocate. Given this runs during startup inside a constructor that already throws on normal startup failures, I would not file that as a defect. No build/server run performed; this was a static review/search pass.
